        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375690",
          "text": "<p>Consider two $2$-bit numbers $A=a_1a_0$ and $B=b_1b_0$. The value of a $2$-bit number $X=x_1x_0$ is defined as:<br>\n$v(X)=x_1\\times 2^1+x_0\\times2^0$<br>\nAssume that A and B are such that $|v(A)-v(B)|\\leq2$. A four-variable function $f(a_1,a_0,b_1,b_0)$ is to have value $1$ whenever $v(A)\\leq v(B)$, and value $0$ otherwise.<br>\nThe number of prime implicants and essential prime implicants for this function $f$, respectively, are</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$5,5$</li>\n\t<li>$5,4$</li>\n\t<li>$6,5$</li>\n\t<li>$6,4$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,prime-implicants,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375707",
          "text": "<p>Gray code for some natural number $n$ is $1111 \\; 1111$ and it is stored in an $8$ -bit register $\\text{R}$. If we store the Gray code of $n+1$ in $\\text{R}$ then what will be the content of $\\mathrm{R}?$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$0000 \\; 0000$</li>\n\t<li>$1010 \\; 1011$</li>\n\t<li>$1111 \\; 1110$</li>\n\t<li>Cannot store gray code of $n+1$ in an $8$ bit register.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,number-representation,one-mark",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375676",
          "text": "How many boolean functions on $4$ Variables are there whose dual is the same as their complement i.e. a function $f$ for which $\\overline{f} = f_{dual}?$",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "256",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,numerical-answers,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375682",
          "text": "<p>$F$ is a boolean function in five boolean variables $a,b,c, d$ and $e$.<br>\n$$F(a,b,c,d,e)=\\sum(0,1,7,8,14,15,16,17,29,30,31)$$<br>\nLet $D$ be the Dual of function $F$. Then which of the following is Not a subset of (true) minterms of $D?$</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$3,\\;4,\\;5,\\;6,\\;27,\\;28$</li>\n\t<li>$7,\\;8$</li>\n\t<li>$3,\\;4,\\;5,\\;6,\\;7,\\;8$</li>\n\t<li>$3,\\;4,\\;7,\\;8,\\;23,\\;24,\\;29$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,dual-function,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375698",
          "text": "<p>Let $f$ be a boolean function on $n$ boolean variables $(x_1, x_2,\\dots,x_n).$ We say a variable $x_i$ is dummy in boolean function $f$ if $f(x_1,\\dots,x_{i-1}, 0,x_{i+1},\\dots,x_n) = f(x_1,\\dots,x_{i-1}, 1,x_{i+1},\\dots,x_n)$ for all the possible values of the other variables(i.e., variables except $x_i$), then the variable $x_i$ is a dummy variable in $f$. A variable $x_k$ is said to be Non-dummy in function $f$ if $x_k$ is not a dummy variable in $f$.</p>\n\n<p>Consider the following statements regarding the minimized expression of the function $f$:</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>A dummy variable is Never present (in original form or complemented form) in any minimized expression of $f$.</li>\n\t<li>A dummy variable is always present (in original form or complemented form) in every minimized expression of $f$.</li>\n\t<li>A dummy variable may be present (in original form or complemented form) in some minimized expression of $f$.</li>\n\t<li>A Non-dummy variable is Always present (in original form or complemented form) in every minimized expression of $f$.</li>\n\t<li>A Non-dummy variable may not be present (in original form or complemented form) in some minimized expression of $f$.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the above statements is True?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>Only $1$</li>\n\t<li>Only $2,4$</li>\n\t<li>Only $1,4$</li>\n\t<li>Only $3,5$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375700",
          "text": "<p>Consider five seats, numbered $0$ to $4,$ arranged in a circle and described by Boolean variables $\\mathrm{i}_{0}$ to $\\mathrm{i}_{4}$. Boolean variable $\\mathrm{i}_{0}$ is true if seat $0$ is occupied and $i_{0}$ is false if the seat is not occupied (no one is sitting in the seat), likewise for $i_{1}, i_{2}, i_{3}$, and $i_{4}$.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following Boolean expressions is true iff at least two people are sitting next to each other and at least one seat is not occupied?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\left(i_{0} i_{1}+i_{1} i_{2}+i_{2} i_{3}+i_{3} i_{4}+i_{4} i_{0}\\right)\\left(\\overline{i_{0} i_{1} i_{2} i_{3} i_{4}}\\right)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\left(i_{0} i_{1}+i_{1} i_{2}+i_{2} i_{3}+i_{3} i_{4}+i_{4} i_{0}\\right)\\left(i_{0} i_{1} i_{2} i_{3} i_{4}\\right)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\left(i_{0} i_{1}+i_{1} i_{2}+i_{2} i_{3}+i_{3} i_{4}+i_{4} i_{0}\\right)$</li>\n\t<li>None</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375709",
          "text": "<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"313\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"192\"></strong><br>\nThe table in the figure above shows the binary-coded-decimal (BCD) representation of the digits $0$ through $9.$ The Boolean expression that represents the set of invalid codes is</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{A} \\vee \\mathrm{BC}$</li>\n\t<li>$A B \\vee C D$</li>\n\t<li>$A B \\vee A C$</li>\n\t<li>$A B \\vee A D$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,number-representation,one-mark",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375696",
          "text": "Let $f$ be a boolean function on $n$ boolean variables $\\left(\\mathrm{X}_1, \\mathrm{X}_2, \\ldots, \\mathrm{X}_{n}\\right)$.\n\nWe say a variable $\\mathbf{X}_{\\mathrm{i}}$ is dummy in boolean function $f$ if\n$$\nf\\left(\\mathbf{X}_1, \\ldots, \\mathbf{X}_{i-1}, 0, \\mathbf{X}_{i+1}, \\ldots, \\mathbf{X}_n\\right)=f\\left(\\mathbf{X}_1, \\ldots, \\mathbf{X}_{i-1}, 1, \\mathbf{X}_{i+1}, \\ldots, \\mathbf{X}_n\\right)\n$$\nfor all the possible values of the other variables(i.e. variables except $\\mathbf{X}_{\\mathrm{i}}),$ then the variable $\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{i}}$ is a dummy variable in $f$.\n\ni.e. a variable $\\mathbf{X}_{\\mathrm{i}}$ is called dummy if, whenever we complement the value of $X_i$ in any row of the truth table of $f$, then the value of $f$ doesn't change.\n\nNumber of boolean functions on $8$ variables $\\left(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_8\\right)$ such that $x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4$ are dummy variables in those functions, is ___________.",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "65536",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,numerical-answers,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375719",
          "text": "<p>Consider the Karnaugh map below for a boolean function $F(x,y,z,w).$<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"350\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"364\"></strong><br>\nWhich of the following is/are an implicant that's neither a prime implicant, nor a minterm of function $F?$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$y w^{\\prime}$</li>\n\t<li>$yzw^{\\prime}$</li>\n\t<li>$x^{\\prime}z^{\\prime}$</li>\n\t<li>$x^{\\prime}yz^{\\prime}w$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,k-map,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375717",
          "text": "<p>Let $A, B, C$ be three boolean variables. $\\oplus$ and $\\odot$ are exclusive-or(ExOr) and exclusive-nor(ExNor) operations respectively.<br>\nConsider the following statements :</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>$(A \\oplus B) \\oplus C=A \\oplus(B \\oplus C)$</li>\n\t<li>$(A \\odot B) \\odot C=A \\odot(B \\odot C)$</li>\n\t<li>$A \\oplus B \\oplus C=A \\odot B \\odot C$</li>\n\t<li>$A \\oplus B \\oplus C=\\overline{(A \\odot B \\odot C)}$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the above statements is/are correct?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$1$ and $3$ only</li>\n\t<li>$2$ and $4$ only</li>\n\t<li>$1,2$ and $3$ only</li>\n\t<li>$1,2$ and $4$ only</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,one-mark",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375674",
          "text": "Consider a $4$ input boolean function $\\mathrm{F}(\\mathrm{X}, \\mathrm{Y}, \\mathrm{Z}, \\mathrm{T})$.\n\nThe minterm $\\mathrm{X}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{Y}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{Z}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{T}^{\\prime}$ is known to be in the Canonical SOP form of $\\mathrm{F}$.\n\nWhat is the maximum number of minterms that the Canonical SOP form of $\\text{F}$ can have such that no simplification is possible (i.e. Canonical SOP form itself is the minimized SOP form)?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "8",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,numerical-answers,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,min-sum-of-products-form,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375692",
          "text": "<p>The literal count of a Boolean expression is the sum of the number of times each literal appears in the expression. For example, the literal count of $(xy+xz' +x'y)$ is $6$. Let $f$ be some fully-specified function on $n$ variables, $n\\geq&nbsp;4$.<br>\nWhich of the following statement is necessarily true for $f$ :</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>The minimised $\\text{SOP}$ (sum of product) and minimised $\\text{POS}$ (product of sum) forms have the same literal count.</li>\n\t<li>The minimised $\\text{POS}$ form has smaller literal count than the minimised $\\text{SOP}$ form.</li>\n\t<li>The minimised $\\text{SOP}$ form has smaller literal count than the minimised $\\text{POS}$ form.</li>\n\t<li>None of the above.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375713",
          "text": "<p>The possible number of Boolean function of $3$ variables $X, Y$ and $Z$ such that $f(X, Y, Z)=f\\left(X^{\\prime}, Y^{\\prime}, Z^{\\prime}\\right)$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$8$</li>\n\t<li>$16$</li>\n\t<li>$64$</li>\n\t<li>$32$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,one-mark",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375694",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\text{R}1$ and $\\text{R}2$ be two $4$–bit registers that store numbers in $1$’s complement form. For the operation $\\text{R}1 + \\text{R}2,$ which one of the following values of $\\text{R}1$ and $\\text{R}2$ gives an arithmetic overflow?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\text{R}1 = 1011$ and $\\text{R}2 = 1110$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{R}1 = 1100$ and $\\text{R}2 = 1010$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{R}1 = 1111$ and $\\text{R}2 = 1000$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{R}1 = 1001$ and $\\text{R}2 = 1111$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,digital-logic,number-representation,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "375715",
          "text": "An $\\text{XOR}$ gate with $7$ variables(inputs) is being developed. Number of different input combinations for which output is $1$?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "64",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2026_cs_dl_tw_1,goclasses-testseries-2026,numerical-answers,goclasses,digital-logic,boolean-algebra,one-mark",
          "category": "Digital Logic"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
